From: Medium1992 <122653016+Medium1992@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 15:58:24 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] XHTTP server: Grow HTTP/2 flow control windows with the
 reader

Go's HTTP/2 grants fixed windows: 1 MiB per stream on the server and 4 MiB
per stream on Go clients. A reader that stops consuming leaves that much
buffered in the process on every stream, so XHTTP holds far more memory
than a raw TCP transport under slow or paused consumers.

The server now rewrites flow control on its HTTP/2 connections. Both
peers start a stream at the 64 KiB window HTTP/2 itself defines, and a
window only grows to twice what its reader consumed over the lowest
recent round trip, measured with PING, up to what the peer really
granted. Uplink credit goes to the client only as the handler reads;
downlink credit goes to the server only while the client's unread data
stays under the cap. Neither peer is modified or ever sees a window
larger than the other granted, and plain HTTP/1.1 passes through.

A stream that runs the server out of window while a client still owes
credit is probed with PING, so clients that return credit only after
half of their own window is consumed are not stalled.
